## 11. Register HttpClient on Server side for prerendering

In the server Program.cs, also register an HttpClient so that during prerendering the pages can call the API. Actually, a better approach for prerendering is to use a server-side service. But since we're using Auto mode, a simpler approach is to register HttpClient on the server too.

Add to server Program.cs after the existing service registrations:
```csharp
builder.Services.AddHttpClient();
builder.Services.AddScoped(sp =>
{
    var client = sp.GetRequiredService<IHttpClientFactory>().CreateClient();
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://localhost");
    return client;
});
```

Actually, this won't work well for prerendering since the server isn't fully started yet. The better approach for Auto mode is to NOT prerender the interactive components (they already have `@rendermode InteractiveAuto` which means they won't prerender by default if set up correctly), OR we can use a direct DB access approach for prerendering.

The simplest working approach: just register the HttpClient in both projects. For the server, we can skip the base address issue by making the pages not prerender their data (they show "Loading..." during prerender and load data after interactive mode kicks in). This is the default behavior since the `OnInitializedAsync` runs after the component becomes interactive.

So for the server, just add:
```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<HttpClient>();
```

This provides a non-functional HttpClient for prerendering (the component will show "Loading..." during prerender), and once WASM takes over, the real HttpClient with the correct base address will be used.

